%%% renders. Format hardening lands with doc.M1-002 (paideia-os plan
%%% §5.3); the M5-001 source lives in the repo under version control
%%% and the release tool ships the compiled `.pdxdoc` alongside the
%%% binary into /pkgs/pdxsock-1.2.0/doc/pdxsock.pdxdoc.
%%%
%%% Section markers: a bare `==== <title>` line opens a section. Cross-
%%% references use `[[cref: <target>]]` where <target> is either a
%%% doc-local section title or a `<tool>#<section>` fully-qualified
%%% reference the reader resolves against /system/doc/. POSIX-difference
%%% annotations use a `[[posix: <difference>]]` inline tag.

==== NAME
pdxsock -- general TCP/UDP client + server (netcat-adjacent) for
paideia-os user-space networking.

==== SYNOPSIS
    pdxsock <host> <port>       # TCP client: connect, forward stdin,
                                # read reply to stdout, exit.
    pdxsock -l <port>           # TCP server: bind, listen (backlog=8),
                                # accept ONE connection, forward
                                # inbound stream to stdout, exit.
    pdxsock -u <host> <port>    # UDP client -- documented STUB at
                                # v1.2.0, exits 3. See [[cref: LIMITATIONS]].
    pdxsock -lu <port>          # UDP server -- refused at v1.2.0.
                                # See [[cref: LIMITATIONS]].

`<host>` is a dotted-quad IPv4 literal (`a.b.c.d`, each octet 0..255).
There is no DNS resolver in the current paideia-os user surface --
callers pass the numeric address directly. `<port>` is a decimal u16
(0..65535).

==== DESCRIPTION
`pdxsock` is a standalone ring-3 ELF -- its own `_start`, no shared
library, no dynamic linker -- that opens one TCP socket (or refuses
into a documented UDP stub) and drives it through a fixed
send/receive shape. It is the netcat-adjacent tool of the R100
user-space networking wave (`design/networking/r100-user-tools-plan.md`
§7); [[cref: pdxcurl#NAME]], [[cref: pdxping#NAME]], and
[[cref: pdxdig#NAME]] are its wave siblings.

Two real dispatch paths are wired at v1.2.0:

- **TCP client** (`pdxsock <host> <port>`): `sys_socket` -> `sys_connect`
  -> one 4 KiB `sys_read` from stdin -> `sys_send` -> loop
  (`sys_recv` -> `sys_write` to stdout) until EOF -> `sys_shutdown`
  -> `sys_exit(0)`.
- **TCP server** (`pdxsock -l <port>`): `sys_socket` -> `sys_bind`
  -> `sys_listen(backlog=8)` -> `sys_accept` -> loop
  (`sys_recv` -> `sys_write` to stdout) until EOF -> `sys_shutdown`
  -> `sys_exit(0)`.

Both paths emit a 48-byte `SockSessionRecord@0.1` via
`sys_semantic_send` (SC+ ID 115) at recv-loop exit, immediately
before `sys_shutdown`, and additionally `sys_write` the same 48
bytes to fd 3 as an opt-in semantic-pipe (parent pre-wires;
pdxsock never opens fd 3 itself) -- see [[cref: RECORD]].

`pdxsock` does NOT implement the netcat `-e` exec-on-connect flag
(a well-known foot-gun). Full-duplex forwarding (netcat's real
contract, `select`/`poll` over stdin+socket) is out of scope at
v1.2.0 -- see [[cref: LIMITATIONS]].

==== OPTIONS

---- -l
Listen mode -- bind + listen + accept + receive-to-stdout, single
connection. Mutually exclusive with the two-positional
`<host> <port>` form. Combined with `-u` (`-lu <port>`) it is
refused, not stubbed -- see [[cref: LIMITATIONS]].

---- -u
UDP mode. At v1.2.0 emits `pdxsock: udp stub` on fd 2 and exits 3
regardless of any other flag. Kernel-side syscalls (sysnos 96/97,
sendto/recvfrom) landed at R93.M2-004 (paideia-os#2052); the hold
is on `libpdx-net`'s wrapper surface stabilising, tracked as
pdxsock#6 (M3-001).

==== EXIT-CODES
     0   OK -- the send/receive session completed and `sys_shutdown`
         + `sys_exit(0)` were reached.
     1   Argv error -- missing positional, bad dotted-quad octet
         (not-a-digit / out-of-range), bad port (empty / not-a-digit
         / >65535).
     2   Usage refusal -- an argv shape the tool does not accept at
         v1.2.0 (e.g. `-lu`).
     3   UDP stub -- `-u` was given; the client body is not wired
         at v1.2.0.
     `>= 32`
         Syscall errno propagated on socket / bind / listen / accept
         / connect refusal. The raw errno is the exit status; the
         diagnostic on fd 2 names which syscall failed
         [[posix: POSIX shell convention shifts errno into 128 +
         signal / 32 + errno; pdxsock returns the raw errno for
         debuggability]].

==== RECORD
Every completed TCP session (client and server) emits one 48-byte
`SockSessionRecord@0.1` (v1.2-B M3-003; pdxsock#8) via two paths at
recv-loop exit, immediately before `sys_shutdown`:

1. `sys_semantic_send` (SC+ ID 115) -- always emitted; kernel-side
   ring receives the record under the schema tag below.
2. `sys_write(fd=3, record, 48)` -- opt-in semantic-pipe emit. The
   parent (shell / launcher / smoke harness) pre-wires fd 3 to a
   pipe / socket / regular file before execve. `pdxsock` never
   opens fd 3 itself; if fd 3 is not open, `sys_write` returns
   `-EBADF` and the emit path silently discards -- absence of a
   consumer is not a session-completion condition.

Failure paths before the recv loop (socket / connect / bind /
listen / accept refusals; usage / bad-port / bad-ip) emit NO record
via either path -- there is no session to describe.

Schema tag: `0x536F636B53657301` (ASCII mnemonic "SockSes" + version
`0x01`; the tag names the record family, not the byte layout --
consumers select layout by the leading magic + version below).

Record layout (little-endian throughout; 48 bytes @align(8)):

    offset  size  field
    ------  ----  ---------------------------------------------
    +0      [u8;8] magic               ("PDXKSOCK" ASCII; the
                                        little-endian u64
                                        `0x4B434F534B584450`)
    +8      u32   version              (SSR_VERSION = 1; consumers
                                        refuse an unknown version)
    +12     u32   flags                (reserved; 0 at v1.2-B)
    +16     u64   bytes_in             (sum of successful sys_recv)
    +24     u64   bytes_out            (sum of successful sys_send;
                                        server-mode is potentially
                                        nonzero at v1.2-A+ -- the
                                        shared pump does sys_send
                                        on stdin bytes)
    +32     u32   peer_ip              (IPv4 peer, host byte order
                                        from sys_connect input;
                                        server-mode = 0 -- see
                                        Field-shape notes)
    +36     u16   peer_port            (TCP peer port; server-mode
                                        = 0 -- the LOCAL bind port
                                        is what the tool holds in
                                        server mode, and the
                                        record's peer_* fields
                                        describe the OTHER end)
    +38     u16   reserved             (0 at v1.2-B)
    +40     u64   duration_ns          (session end - session start,
                                        raw TSC ticks -- see
                                        Field-shape notes)

Field-shape notes:

- The `PDXKSOCK` magic + `version:u32` header is the generic-tool
  record contract from `design/networking/r100-user-tools-plan.md`
  §13.6. A consumer that finds a record beginning with this magic
  parses under the pdxsock schema without an out-of-band schema-
  tag lookup. Future record-shape revisions bump `version`; readers
  that see an unknown version MUST refuse rather than parse.
- `duration_ns` is **raw TSC ticks**, not nanoseconds. The
  schema-shape name is preserved so a re-land is field-preserving
  rather than field-renaming once `sys_clock_now` (a user-facing
  monotonic clock read) lands. Converting ticks -> wall-time ns
  needs a per-host TSC frequency the kernel does not publish yet.
  See [[cref: LIMITATIONS]].
- **Server-mode `peer_ip` is always 0.** `sys_accept` at v1.2-A
  does not thread an out-address (no `accept4` / `getpeername`
  shape exists in the kernel). Record encodes this honestly --
  `peer_ip == 0` combined with `bytes_out > 0` (which server-mode
  now emits after M2-002's shared-pump landing) is the documented
  "server-mode, peer address not observed" state rather than a
  separate presence flag or mode field. The v1.1-B `mode` bit-
  field the 40-byte record carried was DROPPED at v1.2-B M3-003.

`sys_semantic_send`'s return value (0 / -EFAULT / -EINVAL) and the
opt-in fd-3 `sys_write`'s return (n / -EBADF / -EPIPE / etc.) are
BOTH intentionally discarded at the callsite: the connection is
already over, and any failure at this layer is either a marshalling
bug in `src/main.pdx` (ring path) or an absent-consumer default (fd
path) that the tool cannot act on. The sysno-115 handler's
overwrite-oldest ring policy means a downstream consumer backup
does not surface as `PIPE_FULL` at v1 either -- an older record is
silently displaced.

==== LIMITATIONS
This release's stub coverage and honest-scope constraints, spelled
out so a caller does not mistake absence for a bug:

  - **UDP client (`-u <host> <port>`)** is a documented STUB. Emits
    one diagnostic line on fd 2, exits 3, opens no socket. Lands
    real at pdxsock#6 (M3-001) once `libpdx-net`'s sendto/recvfrom
    wrappers stabilise.
  - **UDP server (`-lu <port>`)** is refused, not stubbed. Out of
    scope per `design/networking/r100-user-tools-plan.md` §7.2 --
    needs `recvfrom`-with-peer-address-out-params ABI that
    `libpdx-net` does not thread through yet.
  - **Full-duplex forwarding** (real netcat contract via
    `sys_poll`, sysno 102 landed R95.M3-001 #2080) is out of
    scope at v1.2.0. The client body does one 4 KiB `sys_read`
    from stdin, one `sys_send`, then loops recv->stdout until
    EOF; the server body is receive-only. This is honest as an
    HTTP-request-shape client (send request, read response,
    close) but is NOT the netcat contract [[posix: netcat's
    stdin<->socket interleave is not implemented; the pdxsock
    body is closer to the `curl --data-binary` fire-and-recv
    shape]].
  - **No DNS resolver.** `<host>` MUST be a dotted-quad IPv4
    literal (`a.b.c.d`). There is no `/etc/resolv.conf`, no
    `getaddrinfo`-equivalent, and no `sys_gethostbyname` in the
    paideia-os user surface at this release.
  - **No IPv6.** `sys_connect`'s address wire is IPv4-only in the
    current kernel; IPv6 tracking is escalated per
    `design/networking/ipv6-deferral.md`.
  - **No TLS.** Cleartext TCP only. TLS termination is out of
    tree at this release; see
    `design/networking/tls-placement-decision.md` for the
    layered-tool intent (pdxsock stays plain-TCP; a future
    pdxtls tool wraps it, rather than pdxsock growing a
    `--tls` flag).
  - **`SockSessionRecord@0.1` `duration_ns` is TSC ticks, not
    ns.** See [[cref: RECORD]] for the field-preserving re-land
    plan; blocked on user-facing `sys_clock_now`.
  - **Server-mode `peer_ip` is always 0.** See [[cref: RECORD]]
    for the `sys_accept` out-address extension that unblocks
    this.
  - **No `audit_id` field on `SockSessionRecord@0.1`.**
    `libpdx-audit` integration is pdxsock#8 (M3-002); the
    additive field-land happens there.
  - **Listen fd is not explicitly `sys_close`d before exit.**
    `sys_exit(0)` reaps the whole task and releases every cap
    slot, so the un-closed listen fd is not a leak (matches
    every other `_start` in the paideia-os `src/user/` tree).
  - **No `--dry-run` flag** -- pdxsock#3 (M1-003).
  - **No long-form argv (`--listen` / `--udp` / `--dry-run`)** --
    pdxsock#2 (M1-002). Only the minimal grammar in
    [[cref: SYNOPSIS]] is accepted at v1.2.0.

See [[cref: pdxsock#CHANGELOG]] (this repo's `CHANGELOG.md`) for
the full, itemised list with the exact source-file and issue
number each limitation traces to.

